.TH IN-1 1 "September 2026" "in-1" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
in-1 \- instant dev tools for your current shell


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBsource <(curl -sL in-1.cc)\fP \fITOOL\fP\&... [\fIVAR\fP=\fIVALUE\fP]...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fITOOL\fP\&... [\fIVAR\fP=\fIVALUE\fP]...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--local\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--list\fP | \fB--version\fP | \fB--help\fP

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--env\fP \fISHELL\fP \fITOOL\fP\&...

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB--complete\fP \fISHELL\fP

.PP
\fBin-1\fP \fB-U\fP | \fB--update\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP] [\fITOOL\fP\&...]


.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBin-1\fP installs development tools and languages under a prefix and
puts a single directory, \fI/bin\fP, on \fBPATH\fP for the current
shell session (plus \fBMANPATH\fP and completions).
Nothing else on the system changes.

.PP
Each tool version installs into \fI/share//\fP,
and in-1 writes a small wrapper for every command it provides into
\fI/bin\fP\&.  The wrapper carries the tool's own environment
(CARGO_HOME, ...), so \fBwhich <cmd>\fR is always \fI/bin/\fP
and the shell itself stays clean.  Multiple versions coexist: the
primary command of a tool also gets a version-specific wrapper,
\fI-\fP\&.

.PP
For a session the prefix is \fI$IN1_ROOT/local\fP, where \fIIN1_ROOT\fP is
the in-1 clone itself (\fI/tmp/in-1\fP for the one-liner); for \fB--local\fP
it is \fIPREFIX\fP (default \fI~/.local\fP).

.PP
Before an install, in-1 checks whether its own clone or its makes
clone is behind its origin and prints one line per repo if so.
Nothing is updated unless you ask with \fB-U\fP; set \fIIN1_OFFLINE\fP to
skip the check.

.PP
It works in bash, zsh and fish, on Linux and macOS (Intel and ARM),
and needs only \fBgit\fP(1), \fBcurl\fP(1), GNU \fBmake\fP(1) and
\fBbash\fP(1) to be installed.

.PP
Tools are installed by the makes project
(https://github.com/makeplus/makes); any tool with a \fITOOL\fP\&.mk file
there is available.  A tool may also be requested by an alias command
name (e.g. \fBcargo\fP installs rust, \fBbb\fP installs babashka).
Run \fBin-1 --list\fP to see tools and aliases.

.PP
Any \fIVAR\fP=\fIVALUE\fP argument is passed through to makes as a variable.
Pin a version with the tool's makes variable, for example
\fBin-1 perl PERL-VERSION=5.42.0\fP\&.  A pinned version installs
alongside others and is reachable as both \fIperl\fP and its
version-specific wrapper.  \fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP is the exception: it sets
the install prefix, like the \fIPREFIX\fP environment variable.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--local\fP
  Install the tools under \fIPREFIX\fP (default \fI~/.local\fP, or
  \fI/usr/local\fP when run as root) and write a wrapper for each tool
  command into \fIPREFIX/bin\fP\&.
  These installs persist across shell sessions and need no shell
  setup, since the wrappers carry the tools' environment.

.PP
\fB--list\fP
  List all available tool names and command aliases.

.PP
\fB--env\fP \fISHELL\fP
  Install the tools, then print environment setup code for \fISHELL\fP
  (\fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP or \fBfish\fP) on stdout.
  This is the plumbing used by the sourced one-liner and by the
  \fBin-1\fP shell function; you can use it directly:
  \fBeval "$(in-1 --env bash rust)"\fP

.PP
\fB--complete\fP \fISHELL\fP
  Print tab completion code for the \fBin-1\fP command itself.

.PP
\fB-U\fP, \fB--update\fP
  Update the in-1 clone (\fIIN1_ROOT\fP) and its makes clone before
  doing anything else.
  Alone, that is all it does; with tools or other options it then
  continues with them, so \fBin-1 -U rust\fP gets the newest in-1 and
  makes and then installs rust.
  The in-1 clone moves to the latest default branch (or to
  \fIIN1_VERSION\fP if set) and the one-liner leaves it there from then
  on.

.PP
\fB--version\fP
  Print the in-1 version.

.PP
\fB--help\fP
  Print usage help.


.SH ENVIRONMENT
\fBIN1_ROOT\fP
  The in-1 clone.
  It holds the makes clone (\fImakes/\fP), the session installs
  (\fIlocal/\fP), logs (\fIlog/\fP) and the download cache (\fIcache/\fP).
  Default: the clone the \fBin-1\fP command runs from; the one-liner
  uses \fI/tmp/in-1\fP (\fI$TMPDIR/in-1\fP when \fITMPDIR\fP is set).
  Sourcing \fI\&.rc\fP exports it.

.PP
\fBIN1_CACHE\fP
  Download cache directory.
  Default: \fI$IN1_ROOT/cache\fP\&.

.PP
\fBPREFIX\fP
  Install prefix.
  Session default: \fI$IN1_ROOT/local\fP; \fB--local\fP default:
  \fI~/.local\fP, or \fI/usr/local\fP when root.  A relative path is anchored
  to the current directory.  A \fIPREFIX\fP=\fIDIR\fP argument sets it too.

.PP
\fBIN1_VERSION\fP
  The in-1 git ref to use.

.PP
\fBIN1_REPO\fP
  The in-1 repo URL to clone.
  Default: \fIhttps://github.com/in-1-cc/in-1\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_MAKES_REPO\fP
  The makes repo URL to clone.
  Default: \fIhttps://github.com/makeplus/makes\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_UPDATE\fP
  Set to 1 for the same effect as \fB-U\fP\&.

.PP
\fBIN1_OFFLINE\fP
  Set to 1 to skip the is-it-behind check before installs.

.PP
\fBIN1_VERBOSE\fP
  Set to 1 to stream the full install output instead of the quiet
  per-tool progress lines.

.PP
\fBIN1_TOOLS\fP
  Set by in-1 in the shell; lists the active tools.


.SH EXAMPLES
Try Rust and Node in the current shell:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) rust node
.EE

.PP
Same, from fish:

.EX
curl -sL in-1.cc | source - rust node
.EE

.PP
Pin a version (both \fBgo\fR and \fBgo-1.23.4\fR end up on PATH):

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) go GO-VERSION=1.23.4
.EE

.PP
Install a tool by an alias command name:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) cargo
.EE

.PP
Install the in-1 command permanently:

.EX
git clone https://github.com/in-1-cc/in-1 ~/.in-1
echo 'source ~/.in-1/.rc' >> ~/.bashrc
.EE

.PP
Keep jq around for good:

.EX
in-1 --local jq
.EE

.PP
Update in-1 and makes, then install the newest node:

.EX
in-1 -U node
.EE


